Subject: wasix: let an unset socket timeout mean no deadline

Every socket call that can block read the matching timeout option off
the socket and, when the guest had not set one, substituted thirty
seconds: sock_recv, sock_recv_from, sock_send, sock_send_to,
sock_send_file, sock_accept and the fd_read and fd_write paths for a
socket, plus bind, listen and connect inside the socket itself. Two
things follow from that default, and both are wrong.

The first is guest-visible. A blocking recv on a socket with no
SO_RCVTIMEO set returned ETIMEDOUT after thirty seconds, and a
blocking read of the same socket returned EAGAIN, where Linux blocks
until data arrives. A guest could not ask for the Linux behaviour
either: clearing the option puts None back on the socket, which the
callers turned straight back into thirty seconds. Worse, listen
stored the default on the listener it built, so every later accept on
that listener carried a thirty second deadline the guest never asked
for and could not see coming.

The second is that a deadline is not free. Each of those calls handed
the timeout to a select against tasks.sleep_now, so every blocking
read, write and accept armed a timer in the task manager for an
instant that, for a guest that never sets a socket timeout, cannot be
reached before the operation completes. An event loop pays it on
every socket call it makes.

So pass the option through. The socket operations already take
Option<Duration> and already have a branch that awaits the operation
alone when there is none; bind, listen and connect, which did not,
select against a small deadline future that is simply
std::future::pending when the timeout is absent. An operation with a
timeout set behaves exactly as before, down to the errno.

What that saves depends on the task manager. The one this tree ships
for the sys backend spawns a Tokio task per sleep and aborts it when
the operation wins the race, so every blocking socket call scheduled
and cancelled a task on the runtime; a task manager that polls its
timers in place instead still armed and dropped a timer per call.

Unit tests cover the deadline future arming no timer, and so staying
pending, when no timeout is set, and arming one and expiring when one
is.

